body:
### What problem are you trying to solve?

The canvas element has a bitmap size that can be different than its display size, after CSS is applied to it. To achieve pixel perfect drawing on various pixel density monitors, one has to use a `ResizeObserver`, set the canvas size, then scale the context so that it matches the current pixel density.  
```js
const observer = new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {
  canvas.width = entry.devicePixelContentBoxSize[0].inlineSize;
  canvas.height = entry.devicePixelContentBoxSize[0].blockSize;
  ctx.scale(canvas.width / entry.contentBoxSize[0].inlineSize, canvas.height / entry.contentBoxSize[0].blockSize);
});
observer.observe(canvas, {box: 'device-pixel-content-box'});
```

This is cumbersome: not only is the code verbose, but the `ResizeObserver` callback fires in its own step in the event loop, after animation frames, so we need to either draw twice in a frame or draw at the wrong dimensions for a frame. See this StackOverflow question about this issue: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/77842752/how-do-i-use-resizeobserver-with-requestanimationframe-correctly/77842753#77842753

I expect this to become even more of an issue if/when [the html-in-canvas proposal ](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/11588) comes to life, given its added complexity regarding the rendering of other elements that live in the CSS sizes world where they get the auto scaling to device pixel size.

### What solutions exist today?

The use of a `ResizeObserver` and complex logic to avoid useless redrawings.

### How would you solve it?

I was thinking we could extend the [`reset()`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/canvas.html#dom-context-2d-reset) method of the `CanvasState` mixin so that it accepts a new option bag, where one property, whose name is to be bikeshed, would automatically use the proper values to set the canvas size, and (optionaly?) set the context's scale. 

```webidl
dictionary CanvasResetOptions {
  boolean sizeToDevicePixel = false; // Name to be bikeshed
  boolean setScale = true; // Not sure this is needed
}

interface mixin CanvasState {
 // ...
 reset(optional CanvasResetOptions = {});
 // ...
}
```

This option would have effect only over contexts whose canvases are rendered in DOM, and ignored otherwise (I think it's simpler to ignore placeholder canvases).  

In case it's called before the `ResizeObserver` callbacks have fired, this would cause an early reflow, but I believe this is an acceptable drawback, `reset()` is generally called only once per frame, `onpaint` as currently planned fires after, and other canvas methods also do cause reflows in even more sneaky ways (like setting `.filter` or calling `fillText()`).

